1
00:03:15,555 --> 00:03:21,555
Inequality for All is a documentary film directed by Jacob Kornbluth.

2
00:06:31,111 --> 00:06:37,111
Based on Reich's 2010 book Aftershock: The Next Economy and America's Future, the
film examines widening income inequality in the United States.

3
00:09:46,666 --> 00:09:52,666
Labor Secretary Robert Reich tries to raise awareness
of the country's widening economic gap.

4
00:13:02,222 --> 00:13:08,222
He publicly argued about the issue for decades, and producing a film
of his viewpoints was a "final frontier" for him.

5
00:16:17,777 --> 00:16:23,777
In addition to being a social issue documentary, Inequality for All is also partially a biopic regarding
Reich's early life and his time as Secretary of Labor under Bill Clinton's presidency.

6
00:19:33,333 --> 00:19:39,333
Warren Buffett and Nick Hanauer, two entrepreneurs and investors in the top 1%, are interviewed in the film, supporting
Reich's belief in an economy that benefits all citizens, including those of the middle and lower classes.

7
00:22:48,888 --> 00:22:54,888
Self, played by Robert Reich.

8
00:26:04,444 --> 00:26:10,444
Doralee Rhodes (archive footage), played by Dolly Parton.

9
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:26,000
Mary Beth Lacey (archive footage), played by Tyne Daly.

10
00:32:35,555 --> 00:32:41,555
Violet Newstead (archive footage), played by Lily Tomlin.

11
00:35:51,111 --> 00:35:57,111
Mary Richards (archive footage), played by Mary Tyler Moore.

12
00:39:06,666 --> 00:39:12,666
Murphy Brown (archive footage), played by Candice Bergen.

13
00:42:22,222 --> 00:42:28,222
Inequality for All is a 2013 documentary film directed by Jacob Kornbluth and
narrated by American economist, author and professor Robert Reich.

14
00:45:37,777 --> 00:45:43,777
Based on Reich's 2010 book Aftershock: The Next Economy and America's Future, the
film examines widening income inequality in the United States.

15
00:48:53,333 --> 00:48:59,333
Reich publicly argued about the issue for decades, and producing a
film of his viewpoints was a "final frontier" for him.

16
00:52:08,888 --> 00:52:14,888
In addition to being a social issue documentary, Inequality for All is also partially a biopic regarding
Reich's early life and his time as Secretary of Labor under Bill Clinton's presidency.

17
00:55:24,444 --> 00:55:30,444
Warren Buffett and Nick Hanauer, two entrepreneurs and investors in the top 1%, are interviewed in the film, supporting
Reich's belief in an economy that benefits all citizens, including those of the middle and lower classes.

18
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:46,000
As shown via a series of suspension bridge graphs, the income gap between middle-to-low-class Americans and the top 1% in
the United States was at the same extreme highs in 1928 and 2007, two years that preceded economic crashes.

19
01:01:55,555 --> 01:02:01,555
Reich argues that inequality in capitalism is a necessary incentive for citizens to work harder, but at a low-enough level to where democracy is protected and is in a virtuous cycle;
with high-enough wages and taxes, there will be more investments in government programs, a more college-educated population, and consumer spending creating more jobs.

20
01:05:11,111 --> 01:05:17,111
The United States economy was in this cycle in the 1940s and 1970s, but that changed starting in the late 1970s as a result of union-busting, tax cuts, deregulation, job outsourcing, and other
changes in the system meant to increase Wall Street's profits; this resulted in a decline of average worker pay and an increased amount of average income for top-earners from 1978 to 2010.

21
01:08:26,666 --> 01:08:32,666
Inequality for All premiered at the 2013 Sundance Film Festival in the Documentary Competition section that had
several other political films in its line-up, including another film about income inequality.

22
01:11:42,222 --> 01:11:48,222
It won the festival's U.S.

23
01:14:57,777 --> 01:15:03,777
Documentary Special Jury Award for Achievement in Filmmaking and was
bought by RADiUS-TWC five days after its January 19 premiere.

24
01:18:13,333 --> 01:18:19,333
After months of running at several other festivals, the film was released to United States theaters by RADiUS on September
27, 2013 and grossed more than $1 million in just over a month, which was rare for an issue documentary.

25
01:21:28,888 --> 01:21:34,888
It received very high opinions from professional critics, who praised its easy-to-understand demonstration of a complicated topic and likeable narrator; however, it also garnered
criticism for what libertarian and right-leaning sources and publications deemed to be narrow-minded, unoriginal viewpoints and lack of credible opposing arguments.

26
01:24:44,444 --> 01:24:50,444
The film explores themes of upper class, middle
class, economics, inequality, poor.
